                  "title": "Migration Parity",
                  "body": "When a change moves, splits, extracts, replaces, or externalizes existing behavior, the draft/spec shall define migration parity before implementation. It shall inventory the existing public behavior being moved or replaced; list affected user-facing commands, APIs, hooks, config entries, generated artifacts, and side effects; map each retained behavior to its new owner or an explicit decision to remove it; define acceptance criteria proving retained behavior still works through the new path; and include at least one behavior-level verification for each retained public surface. Registration, discovery, help output, or mock routing alone are not sufficient evidence of migration parity. Intentional behavior removal shall state the user-visible impact and compatibility expectation.\n\nViolation: missing migration parity definition\nDiff-verification conditions:\n- The diff moves, splits, extracts, replaces, or externalizes existing behavior.\n- The draft/spec lacks an inventory of the existing public behavior being moved or replaced.\n- The draft/spec lacks a mapping from each retained behavior to its new owner or an explicit removal decision.\n- The draft/spec relies only on registration, discovery, help output, or mock routing checks for parity evidence.\nBlocking when: retained public behavior lacks behavior-level verification through the new path, or an intentional removal lacks user-visible impact and compatibility expectations.\nAdvisory when: the migration inventory and mapping exist, but an additional retained surface would benefit from clearer acceptance wording without changing the implementation target.",
